<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:23 Political News, Business News</p><h3>Kyiv won’t turn into dead city - energy expert</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – Kyiv can fully avoid becoming a dead, frozen city because energy workers prepared for any scenario, as the speed of power restoration after Russian strikes shows, Center for Energy Research Director Oleksandr Kharchenko said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“Right now this is a scenario Russia is carrying out. It is not a horror, the situation is under control. What is happening is clear. How to fix it is clear. And it will keep repeating as long as massive missile and drone strikes are carried out… Without the ability to restore quickly, we would face a long prospect of bad days… There will be no dead-city situation,” Kharchenko said on the Tse Baza podcast on YouTube.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>After each Russian strike on the capital there will be difficult periods when, on frosty days, electricity is available only six to eight hours a day, he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Kharchenko said he is convinced that “Russia will not manage to knock Kyiv down to worse than six to eight hours with electricity.”</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The capital, which lost its three main combined heat and power plants to ballistic strikes in January, will be supplied from external sources through power lines that the Russians cannot disable, for certain reasons, and from new generation, Kharchenko said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“About 250 MW [of new capacity] will be working. That will not be enough for households, which will come after heat, water and sewage. But I do not believe in collapse, I see how much and what has been done,” he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“A sewage problem for three to four hours is not a problem,” he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>If there is no shelling, restrictions in Kyiv could be lifted in about a week and a half, Kharchenko said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>A mild but realistic scenario for Kyiv under Russian shelling includes periodic emergency power outages every two to three weeks, plus scheduled restrictions of four to six hours a day in between, he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>A harsh scenario means up to 18 hours without electricity in scheduled outages and at times no water, he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“I think we will not reach serious loss of heating, although that is possible too, but not for the whole city and not for long. There will be restoration, and mobile boiler houses and generators,” he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Among other cities that Russia constantly shells, Odesa is at risk this winter because it has no generation of its own but is home to many people, Kharchenko said. Kryvy Rih and Dnipro have generation, but it is under threat of being lost to shelling, he said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Consumption in Chernihiv and Sumy is much lower, but they are even more vulnerable to shelling, he said. The situation in Zaporizhia is better, in his view, because it has a large hydropower plant and no large concentration of thermal sources. In Kharkiv, “a lot has been done,” he said.</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:24 Business News</p><h3>Russian strike hits Roshen candy plant in Vinnytsia</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – Roshen’s production complex in Vinnytsia was hit by a Russian strike on Monday, and a timely alert and quick evacuation saved the lives of employees, the confectionery corporation said on its website.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“Against the backdrop of the attack, Russian propaganda is spreading false claims about alleged ‘drone production’ at the corporation’s enterprises. Roshen officially denies this information,” it said in the release.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Roshen said its enterprises make confectionery products and do not produce drones or other military products.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“Spreading false information does not change the civilian purpose of Roshen’s enterprises and cannot serve as a justification for strikes on them. The corporation stresses the need to investigate the attack and to assess the actions of those responsible for it properly under the law,” it said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>In late August, Russian attack drones hit Roshen’s logistics center in Kyiv, and in July the enemy destroyed the corporation’s former office in the capital.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The Ukrainian corporation Roshen ranked among the world’s 20 largest confectionery companies in 2025, in 13th place, according to the analytics company Global Growth Insights. Its annual revenue reached $1.5 billion, with a compound annual growth rate of 4%, according to the published data.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Vinnytsia Confectionery Factory, part of Roshen, reported net profit of UAH 76.17 million in the first half of 2026, compared with a net loss of UAH 72.26 million a year earlier. Its revenue rose 38.9% to UAH 1.934 billion. Vinnytsia Dairy Plant Roshen increased net profit by 4.2% to UAH 194.7 million in the first half of 2026, while revenue fell 4.1% to UAH 2.373 billion.</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:33 Political News, Business News</p><h3>EU officially announces fourth Ukraine-EU intergovt conference to be held on Oct 13</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – The European Union has officially announced that the fourth conference regarding Ukraine's accession to the European Union—the so-called intergovernmental conference—will take place on Tuesday.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The corresponding announcement was issued by the press service of the Council of the EU on Monday.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“The fourth meeting of the Accession Conference with Ukraine will be held in Luxembourg on 13 October 2026. The meeting will serve to open negotiations on clusters 2 - internal market and 3 – competitiveness and inclusive growth,” the message reads.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The meeting will begin at 18:30 (19:30 Kyiv time), and a press conference is scheduled for 19:30 (20:30 Kyiv time).</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Ukraine will be represented by Vsevolod Chentsov, Deputy Prime Minister for European and Euro-Atlantic Integration.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The first intergovernmental conference took place on June 25, 2025, and focused on the opening of negotiations. The second was held on June 15 of this year, marking the opening of the first cluster—Fundamentals—while the third conference took place on July 14, during which the sixth cluster—Foreign Policy—was opened.</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:38 Business News</p><h3>IMF cuts Azerbaijan 2026 GDP growth forecast to 1.7%</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – The International Monetary Fund forecasts Azerbaijan’s GDP growth in 2026 at 1.7%, according to its October World Economic Outlook, published on Tuesday.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>In April, the IMF forecast the country’s economy would grow 2.2% this year.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The fund expects Azerbaijan’s GDP to grow 2.3% in 2027 (April forecast: growth of 2.5%).</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The IMF forecasts that average annual inflation in the country will accelerate to 5.8% in 2026 (April forecast: 6%) from 5.6% in 2025. The fund’s analysts expect inflation to slow to 5.6% in 2027 (April forecast: 5.1%).</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Azerbaijan’s current account surplus is expected at 11.3% of GDP in 2026 (previous forecast: 9.7% of GDP). The estimate for 2027 is a surplus of 9.8% of GDP (previous forecast: 5.4% of GDP).</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Azerbaijan’s Economy Ministry forecasts GDP growth of 1.7% in 2026 and 2.7% in 2027, with average annual inflation of 5.7% and 5.3% respectively.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Azerbaijan’s economy grew 1.4% in 2025, and the current account surplus was 4.6% of GDP.</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:39 Business News</p><h3>Sumykhimprom sale fails again for lack of bidders</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – An auction to sell the state’s 99.9952% stake in Sumykhimprom, one of Ukraine’s largest chemical companies, did not take place because there were no buyers. It had been scheduled for October 13 in the Prozorro.Sale trading system with a starting price of UAH 1.005 billion.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“The asset is complex, with a heavy background, but for an investor in agrochemicals or building materials it is a ready site for building a national champion,” State Property Fund head Dmytro Natalukha said on Facebook on the eve of the auction.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>A repeat online auction scheduled for January 13, 2026, with the price cut 9.3% to UAH 1.088 billion (excluding VAT), also failed for lack of participants, as did the previous auction on June 11, 2025.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>On July 8 this year the government approved the terms and a starting price of UAH 1.005 billion for the stake.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Under the terms, the new owner had to keep the company’s main activities and invest at least UAH 150 million in technical re-equipment and modernization of production. The buyer also had to repay wage arrears, debts to the budget and overdue payables within six months, observe employees’ social guarantees under labor law and not lay anyone off for six months after the purchase.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The fund will offer several more big and interesting assets at auction next week, Natalukha said. First, the Odesa Portside Plant will go on sale on October 19.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“It is a pearl of Ukrainian industry, but also a monument to corruption that has survived five failed auctions and endless backroom games. It is the biggest and practically unique ammonia and urea transshipment hub on the Black Sea, with its own sea berths, terminals and direct access to deep water. It is a ready, world-class logistics and production hub,” he said, promoting it.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Two other assets, transferred to the state under the law on sanctions against Russians, are the Demurynsky Mining and Processing Plant and Motordetal-Konotop. Their auctions are scheduled for October 20.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Sumykhimprom is one of Ukraine’s largest producers of complex mineral fertilizers, titanium dioxide, sulfuric acid and other inorganic chemicals. It is one of the three biggest budget contributors in Sumy and the region, and it makes more than 30 grades of NPK fertilizers with different nutrient ratios for different soil and climate zones.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>For more than 10 years the plant was managed by a group of companies linked to Group DF of entrepreneur Dmytro Firtash. In November 2023 the Sumy Region Commercial Court granted a motion by the State Property Fund and the Justice Ministry and closed the bankruptcy and rehabilitation proceedings against Sumykhimprom.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The fund planned to sell the company to a private investor even before the full-scale war, but privatization dragged on because of the position of Firtash, a minority shareholder who received 0.005% of the shares in 2010 and control of management. Sumykhimprom piled up debts, which made it possible to form a creditors’ committee and start rehabilitation proceedings.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The fund tried through the courts to end the bankruptcy proceedings from 2015 but succeeded only in 2023, which unblocked privatization.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Russian forces shelled the plant in March 2022, causing an ammonia leak. The plant stood idle for a year and resumed operations in spring 2023. As of June 2025 the front line was less than 30 km from Sumykhimprom.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>According to its 2025 report, the company’s payables at the end of the year were UAH 4.135 billion, of which UAH 1.292 billion was debt in the bankruptcy case opened by the Sumy Region Commercial Court in October 2011, formed under the court-approved register of creditors.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“The lack of working capital, loss-making operations and plant downtime cause growth of payables,” the report said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Sumykhimprom’s 2025 revenue was UAH 129.2 million (UAH 395.9 million a year earlier), gross profit UAH 53.3 million (UAH 9.9 million) and net loss UAH 405.7 million (UAH 600.2 million).</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“The main reason for Sumykhimprom’s losses in 2025 is the forced downtime of production capacity from November 2024 to July 22, 2025 and the downtime of all structural units in the fourth quarter of 2025. In 2025, sulfuric acid shop No. 5 worked for only two months,” the report said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Because of the worsening dangerous situation in the region, the regional military administration did not give permission to buy ammonia, which kept the company from launching its complex mineral fertilizer program as set out in the approved financial plan for 2025, the report said.</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:40 Страхування, Ділові новини, Головнi новини, Транспорт, Фондовий ринок</p><h3>Чергова атака БПЛА була вночі на торгові судна поблизу Болгарії – ЗМІ</h3><p class=MsoNormal>Київ. 13 жовтня. ІНТЕРФАКС-УКРАЇНА – Торгове судно Chelsea 3, яке прямувало під прапором Мальти і зареєстроване в порту Одеси, було атаковане трьома повітряними безпілотниками приблизно за 15 морських миль від міста Бяла, повідомив прем'єр-міністр Румен Радєв, інформує Болгарское национальное радио.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>"Сигнал про інцидент надійшов о 01:30 год. Усіх 15 осіб екіпажу (7 громадян України та 8 – Азербайджану) евакуйовано до Варни. П'ятьом постраждалим морякам надали медичну допомогу", – йдеться у повідомленні.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Chelsea 3 виходив із турецького чорноморського порту Гіресун до румунського порту Джурджулешти, а вантаж, згідно з декларацією, призначався для Молдови. 136-метрове судно, що отримало крен, нині дрейфує без екіпажу навпроти мису Святий Атанас, повідомляє кореспондент Болгарського національного радіо у Варні Данієла Стойнева.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>6 жовтня в цьому ж районі два торгові судна були вражені повітряними та морськими безпілотниками у винятковій економічній зоні Болгарії у Чорному морі, приблизно за 70 морських миль на схід від Бяли. Судно ALFA WATAN, що прямувало під прапором Того, затонуло, а його екіпаж із 12 громадян Сирії зник безвісти. Друге судно – ABLE, під прапором Палау, – зазнало пошкоджень, після чого на ньому спалахнула пожежа.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>"Чергова атака на торгові судна в безпосередній близькості від територіальних вод Болгарії є абсолютно неприпустимою", – заявив прем'єр-міністр Радєв.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Просто під час екстреного засідання Ради безпеки при Раді міністрів надійшла інформація про ще одне атаковане судно. Воно затонуло приблизно за 17 морських миль на схід від с. Крапець. За попередніми даними, одного з членів екіпажу врятовано.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Про це поінформовано постійних представників Болгарії в НАТО і ЄС. "У мене буде розмова з генеральним секретарем НАТО Марком Рютте", – повідомив Радєв. Він також заявив, що викличе посла Росії у Софії Елеонору Митрофанову.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Джерело: https://bnrnews.bg/radiobulgaria/post/541046/novi-dva-targovski-koraba-sa-udareni-kray-balgarskata-akvatoriya</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:40 Business News</p><h3>EU sets Oct 13 for 4th Ukraine accession conference</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – The European Union has officially announced the fourth conference on Ukraine’s accession to the EU, the so-called intergovernmental conference, for Tuesday.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The Council of the EU press service issued the announcement on Monday.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“The fourth meeting of the accession conference with Ukraine will take place in Luxembourg on October 13, 2026. At this meeting, negotiations on clusters 2 (‘Internal Market’) and 3 (‘Competitiveness and Inclusive Growth’) will be opened,” it said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The meeting will begin at 6:30 pm (7:30 pm Kyiv time), and a press conference is scheduled for 7:30 pm (8:30 pm Kyiv time).</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Ukraine will be represented by Deputy Prime Minister for European and Euro-Atlantic Integration Vsevolod Chentsov.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>The first intergovernmental conference, on June 25, 2025, was devoted to the opening of negotiations. The second, on June 15 this year, opened the first cluster, Fundamentals, and the third, on July 14, opened the sixth cluster, external relations.</p>
<hr/><p>13.10.2026 09:41 Business News</p><h3>Gas back on in Pryluky after Russian strike - Naftogaz</h3><p class=MsoNormal>KYIV. Oct 13 (Interfax-Ukraine) – The Naftogaz group has fully restored gas supply in Pryluky (Chernihiv region) after the Russian attack on October 7, the company said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“Specialists eliminated the damage, checked the gas pipelines and equipment, and returned gas to all 18,000 homes in stages,” the group said in a Facebook post on Monday.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>“45 emergency crews worked to restore gas supply to all consumers as quickly as possible. I thank the specialists of Gas Networks for their professional work and responsibility,” Naftogaz of Ukraine acting management board chairman Serhiy Fedorenko said.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>Russia struck Naftogaz group gas production facilities in Kharkiv region (Ukrgasvydobuvannya) overnight on October 12, causing a fire and damaging equipment, but no one was hurt.</p>
<p class=MsoNormal>On October 7, an overnight attack on Pryluky damaged a medium-pressure gas pipeline, and more than 18,000 residents were temporarily left without gas. A woman and a man who worked for the Naftogaz group died in a house destroyed by a missile.</p>
